Migrating from Laravel Pint
***************************

{{description: Migrate from Laravel Pint to DressCode: the laravel standard carries the style of Pint's laravel preset, migrating takes one line of configuration, and the same run also upgrades your code to newer PHP and Laravel.}}

.[perex]
Laravel Pint formats code with its `laravel` preset, and the `laravel` standard of DressCode has the same style. Migrating takes one line of configuration, which `dresscode init` writes by itself in a project with Pint, and the same run that formats your code can also upgrade it to a newer PHP and Laravel.


Why DressCode Instead of Pint
=============================

Pint is a command-line tool of its own, built on PHP CS Fixer, with its own presets. The default one, `laravel`, is a list of fixers it runs on your code. DressCode carries that list as the `laravel` standard, so it leaves code that Pint formatted almost unchanged. This was verified on fourteen Laravel packages, 1,615 files run through Pint 1.32: `dresscode check --use laravel` found 129 places in them, and each of them is one of the differences described below (October 2026).

What you get in addition:

- **Upgrading in the same run.** The `modernizations` set rewrites code to the constructs of a newer PHP version, `deprecations` fixes what PHP has deprecated, and the `dresscode/upgrade-laravel` package rewrites the API that Laravel changed between versions. See [Upgrading to a Newer PHP |upgrading-php] and [Laravel |upgrading-laravel].
- **Class names in phpDoc are imported in every tag**, even in `@template` and `@method`, where Pint leaves them fully qualified.
- **Output for AI agents and editors.** The `agent` format tells an AI agent what changed in a file after the agent edited it, so that its next edit does not fail. See [AI agents |agents].


Converting
==========

In a project that requires `laravel/pint` or has a `pint.json`, [`dresscode init` |cli#init] writes the standard that matches the Pint preset by itself; for the default `laravel` preset, it prints:

/--pre .[terminal]
Standard   laravel (the style of Laravel Pint), Laravel Pint formats the project in this style
\--

A configuration written by hand is one line:

```neon
use: laravel
```

If your `pint.json` has extra rules in its `rules` key on top of the preset, `dresscode migrate config` translates them together with the preset, because they are rules of PHP CS Fixer; it lists what has no counterpart, see [Migrating to DressCode |migration#migrate-config]. You can also look them up yourself: `dresscode catalogue` lists, for every decision, the PHP CS Fixer fixers it covers, so you will find `no_unused_imports` as `imports.unused`. `migrate config` writes the `exclude` key to `excludePaths`, see [Configuration |configuration#Paths].

| Pint | DressCode |
|---|---|
| `pint` | `dresscode fix` |
| `pint --test` | `dresscode check` |
| `pint app/Models` | `dresscode fix app/Models` |
| `pint --preset laravel` | `dresscode fix --use laravel` |


Where DressCode Differs from Pint
=================================

On code that Pint formatted, you will run into these differences:

- DressCode shortens and imports a class name in a doc comment in every tag; Pint does it only in the tags its `fully_qualified_strict_types` lists, which means not in `@template` or `@method`.
- DressCode merges two blank lines in a row inside a doc comment into one. Pint trims only its start and end.
- DressCode removes an import whose name appears only in the text of a comment, as unused. Pint keeps it.
- In a comparison of a literal with a constant, `'\\' === DIRECTORY_SEPARATOR`, DressCode puts the constant on the left. Pint leaves both sides as they are.
- Line endings follow the majority in the file (`file.lineEnding: majority`), so a project on Windows with CRLF stays with CRLF. Pint writes LF.

And here is what Pint does and DressCode does not do yet: renaming test methods to snake_case (`php_unit_method_casing`), sorting traits (`ordered_traits`), and aligning or sorting phpDoc tags (`phpdoc_align`, `phpdoc_order`, `phpdoc_separation`). The page [Standards and Sets |presets-reference#laravel] shows the whole preset, with comments.
